## HETEROGENEOUS `+` — `impl NumAdd Int for Float`, shipped by
## "feat(abilities): class-parameter NumAdd for heterogeneous `+`".
##
## `NumAdd Float for Float` and `NumAdd Int for Float` share a receiver and
## differ ONLY in the operand, so the full positional tuple is the only thing
## that can choose between them. Both call-site emitters built that tuple by
## keeping just the VARIABLE positions, so `NumAdd Int Float` arrived as the
## 1-tuple `[Int]`: too short to match a 2-annotation impl, and short enough to
## read as a single-parameter ability, which switched off the B1 guard that
## exists to stop receiver-only matching. What was left matched on whichever
## var survived — the OPERAND here — picking `impl NumAdd Int for Int`:
##
##   error[T0201]: Type mismatch for associated type 'out':
##                 expected Int but got Float
##
## The direction matters, and it is the evidence for the diagnosis:
##
##   n + 0.5    `NumAdd Float 'a`   first var IS the receiver   -> right, by luck
##   0.5 + n    `NumAdd 'a Float`   first var is the OPERAND    -> wrong impl
##
## `HeterogeneousNumericDispatchTest` is the regression test.
##
## STILL OPEN, and this file does not cover it: a heterogeneous add reached
## through a DICTIONARY — a polymorphic HOF — still dies with
##
##   UnsupportedSpecializationException … CallNativeDDD [FloatOps$$Lambda, 0.5, 21]
##
## It is NOT enough to make the dictionary lookup tuple-aware. Tried, measured,
## reverted: `resolveOpFromObligation` already matches a tuple position by
## position, and feeding it the callee's constraint tuple changes nothing here,
## because the tuple is not CONCRETE at that point. Probing the two sites of one
## HOF (`apply` at an Int accumulator and at a Float one) prints
##
##   typeArgs=[VarType(out, id=1983), Float]   bindings=[]
##   typeArgs=[VarType(out, id=1983), Int]     bindings=[]
##
## The operand position is the CALLBACK'S RESULT TYPE, and it is the SAME VAR
## `id=1983` at both sites. One variable cannot be Int at one call and Float at
## another, so it can never resolve — the callee's ability-constraint var was
## not freshened per call site. Lowering is then left with the receiver alone
## and necessarily picks the homogeneous impl.
##
## The same unresolved var is why mono cannot specialise the HOF and
## `apply_fold` keeps `__dict_add` (see `bench/cases/defunc` kernel 3 vs 7).
## One cause, a correctness bug and the headline perf gap.
use std::effects::log::{ println, Console }
use std::float

## Receiver CONCRETE (Float), operand a VAR the call site pins to Int.
let direct = { n } -> 0.5 + n

## The other direction: receiver a var, operand concrete.
let other = { n } -> n + 0.5

pub let main = {} -> perform Console in do
  ## Both 21.5. The CONSUMER is the trigger: `float::to_str` pins `out` to
  ## Float before the resolution runs. Without it `out` stays open, nothing
  ## conflicts, and the wrong impl goes unnoticed.
  println { msg = float::to_str { n = direct { n = 21 } } }
  println { msg = float::to_str { n = other { n = 21 } } }
